* Instead of using an absolute value (`abs`) to determine the direction, we can simply check whether the interval is positive or negative.

Here's an updated version with optimizations:
```cpp
void cycleLEDs(int interval) {
  const int nOutPins = sizeof(outPins) / sizeof(outPins[0]);
  static unsigned long lastChange = 0;
  static int currIndex = 0; // Use static to retain value between function calls

  if (millis() >= lastChange + abs(interval)) {
    bool forward = interval > 0;
    int nextIndex = (currIndex + (forward ? 1 : -1)) % nOutPins;

    // Turn off all LEDs except the current and next ones
    for (int i = 0; i < nOutPins; i++) {
      digitalWrite(outPins[i], (i == currIndex || i == nextIndex) ? HIGH : LOW);
    }

    // Update current index
    currIndex = nextIndex;

    // Update timer
    lastChange = millis();
  }
}
```
This version reduces the number of array accesses and simplifies the logic for determining the direction.

**Size**

