Sure! Let's start by creating the `FavoritesManager` class which will handle the logic for managing favorite business names. We'll also set up the `FavoritesController` to interact with this manager.

### Step 1: Create the `FavoritesManager` Class

First, let's define the methods needed in the `FavoritesManager` class:

- `addFavorite($name)`: Adds a name to the favorites list.
- `removeFavorite($name)`: Removes a name from the favorites list.
- `getFavorites()`: Retrieves the current list of favorites.

Here is how you can implement these methods:

```php
// app/Favorites/FavoritesManager.php

namespace App\Favorites;

use App\Models\User;
use App\Models\Favorite;
use Illuminate\Support\Facades\Session;

class FavoritesManager
{
    protected $userId;

    public function __construct()
    {
        if (Auth::check()) {
            $this->userId = Auth::id();
        } else {
            // For guests, use session storage
            $this->userId = 'guest';
        }
    }

    public function addFavorite($name)
    {
        if ($this->userId === 'guest') {
            Session::push('favorites', $name);
        } else {
            Favorite::updateOrCreate(
                ['user_id' => $this->userId, 'name' => $name],
                ['user_id' => $this->userId]
            );
        }
    }

    public function removeFavorite($name)
    {
        if ($this->userId === 'guest') {
            Session::forget('favorites.' . array_search($name, Session::get('favorites')));
        } else {
            Favorite::where('user_id', $this->userId)->where('name', $name)->delete();
        }
    }

    public function getFavorites()
    {
        if ($this->userId === 'guest') {
            return Session::get('favorites', []);
        } else {
            return Favorite::where('user_id', $this->userId)->pluck('name')->toArray();
        }
    }
}
```

### Step 2: Create the `FavoritesController`

Next, let's create the `FavoritesController` to expose API endpoints for interacting with the `FavoritesManager`.

```php
// app/Http/Controllers/FavoritesController.php

namespace App\Http\Controllers;

use App\Favorites\FavoritesManager;
use Illuminate\Http\Request;

class FavoritesController extends Controller
{
    protected $favoritesManager;

    public function __construct(FavoritesManager $favoritesManager)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager = $favoritesManager;
    }

    public function index()
    {
        return response()->json(['favorites' => $this->favoritesManager->getFavorites()]);
    }

    public function store(Request $request)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager->addFavorite($request->input('name'));
        return response()->json(['message' => 'Name added to favorites']);
    }

    public function destroy(Request $request)
    {
        $this->favoritesManager->removeFavorite($request->input('name'));
        return response()->json(['message' => 'Name removed from favorites']);
    }
}
```

### Step 3: Define Routes

Finally, let's define the routes in your `routes/api.php` file to map the controllers to their respective actions.

```php
// routes/api.php

use App\Http\Controllers\FavoritesController;

Route::middleware('auth:sanctum')->group(function () {
    Route::get('/favorites', [FavoritesController::class, 'index']);
    Route::post('/favorites', [FavoritesController::class, 'store']);
    Route::delete('/favorites/{name}', [FavoritesController::class, 'destroy']);
});
```

This setup provides a basic structure for handling favorites both for authenticated users and guests. The `FavoritesManager` class manages the data, while the `FavoritesController` exposes the necessary API endpoints. You can now proceed to build the Vue frontend to consume these APIs.

