To extract hashtags from a given text in C#, you can use regular expressions (regex). Here's an example of how to do it:

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        string inputText = "This is a sample text with #hashtags and #moreHashtags.";

        // Define the regex pattern for matching hashtags
        string hashtagPattern = @"#\w+";

        // Use Regex.Matches to find all matches in the input text
        MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(inputText, hashtagPattern);

        // Iterate through the matches and print them
        foreach (Match match in matches)
        {
            Console.WriteLine(match.Value);
        }
    }
}
```

In this code:
- The `inputText` variable contains the text from which you want to extract hashtags.
- The `hashtagPattern` variable defines the regex pattern that matches any word starting with a `#`.
- The `Regex.Matches` method finds all occurrences of the pattern in the input text.
- Finally, we iterate over the `MatchCollection` and print each matched hashtag.

When you run this program, it will output:
```
#hashtags
#moreHashtags
```

This approach ensures that only valid hashtags are extracted from the text.

To add the functionality to the button, you can use JavaScript (or jQuery if you prefer) to handle the click event and make an AJAX request to your API endpoint. Here's how you can do it:

### Using Plain JavaScript

```html
<script>
document.querySelector('.btn-primary').addEventListener('click', function() {
    const userName = document.getElementById('user_name').value;
    const userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

    fetch(`http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`, {
        method: 'POST',
        headers: {
            'Content-Type': 'application/json'
        }
    })
    .then(response => response.json())
    .then(data => {
        console.log('Success:', data);
        // Optionally, update the table with the new user information
        addUserToTable(userName, userKey);
    })
    .catch((error) => {
        console.error('Error:', error);
    });
});

function addUserToTable(userName, userKey) {
    const tableBody = document.querySelector('#userTable tbody');
    const newRow = document.createElement('tr');

    const userCell = document.createElement('td');
    userCell.textContent = userName;

    const userKeyCell = document.createElement('td');
    userKeyCell.textContent = userKey;

    const permissionsCell = document.createElement('td');
    permissionsCell.textContent = 'Admin'; // You can set this dynamically or leave it blank

    const operationCell = document.createElement('td');
    const deleteButton = document.createElement('button');
    deleteButton.className = 'btn btn-danger';
    deleteButton.textContent = 'Delete';
    deleteButton.addEventListener('click', () => deleteUserFromTable(userName));

    operationCell.appendChild(deleteButton);

    newRow.appendChild(userCell);
    newRow.appendChild(userKeyCell);
    newRow.appendChild(permissionsCell);
    newRow.appendChild(operationCell);

    tableBody.appendChild(newRow);
}

function deleteUserFromTable(userName) {
    const row = Array.from(document.querySelectorAll('#userTable tbody tr')).find(row => 
        row.querySelector('td').textContent === userName
    );
    if (row) {
        row.remove();
    }
}
</script>
```

### Using jQuery

If you prefer using jQuery, you can simplify the code:

```html
<script src="https://code.jquery.com/jquery-3.6.0.min.js"></script>
<script>
$(document).ready(function() {
    $('.btn-primary').on('click', function() {
        const userName = $('#user_name').val();
        const userKey = 'd2db5ec4-6e7a-11ee-b962-0242ac120002';

        $.ajax({
            url: `http://localhost:5000/api/users/${userName}/devices/${userKey}`,
            type: 'POST',
            success: function(data) {
                console.log('Success:', data);
                addUserToTable(userName, userKey);
            },
            error: function(error) {
                console.error('Error:', error);
            }
        });
    });

    function addUserToTable(userName, userKey) {
        const tableBody = $('#userTable tbody');
        const newRow = $('<tr>');

        newRow.append($('<td>').text(userName));
        newRow.append($('<td>').text(userKey));
        newRow.append($('<td>').text('Admin')); // You can set this dynamically or leave it blank

        const deleteButton = $('<button>', {
            class: 'btn btn-danger',
            text: 'Delete',
            click: function() {
                deleteUserFromTable(userName);
            }
        });

        newRow.append($('<td>').append(deleteButton));

        tableBody.append(newRow);
    }

    function deleteUserFromTable(userName) {
        $('tr').each(function() {
            if ($(this).find('td:first').text() === userName) {
                $(this).remove();
            }
        });
    }
});
</script>
```

### Explanation

1. **JavaScript**:
   - The `fetch` API is used to send a POST request to the server.
   - The `addUserToTable` function adds a new row to the table with the user's name and key.
   - The `deleteUserFromTable` function removes a user from the table when the delete button is clicked.

2. **jQuery**:
   - The `$.ajax` method is used to send a POST request to the server.
   - The `addUserToTable` function creates a new row in the table and appends it.
   - The `deleteUserFromTable` function finds and removes the corresponding row from the table.

Both approaches achieve the same result, so you can choose the one that best fits your project.

